{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790254289,"nonce":"xOss6dIWTbLVu+BeJct4OBH+C9E=","title":"Lua: a raise in a loop or if condition reports a stale line","message":"The line an error carries is stored at the start of each statement that has a check, and restored after each checked call. A compound statement (`while`, `if`, `repeat ... until`, a generic `for`'s iterator call) stores none for its own header: lowering its body resets the flag that asks for the store. A raise in the header then carries whatever line was stored last: the statement before the loop on the first iteration, the body's last checked statement after that.\n\nReproducer (lua5.4 says line 4 for both):\n\n    local function g(x) if x \u003e 100 then error(\"big\") end return x end\n    local y = nil\n    local x = g(1)\n    while x \u003c y do x = g(x) end\n\nreports line 3.\n\n    local function g(x) if x \u003e 100 then error(\"big\") end return x end\n    local y = 5\n    local x = g(1)\n    while x \u003c y do\n      x = g(x) + 1\n      if x == 3 then y = nil end\n    end\n\nreports line 5. The same holds for `if` and `elseif` conditions. The fix is a line store before a header that can raise, on every evaluation of it.","files":null}]}